WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.437 --> 00:00:02.337
Narrator: See these hairs on Sully's arm

00:00:02.337 --> 00:00:03.993
from "Monsters, Inc."?

00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:06.770
Instead of animating each hair one by one,

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:10.490
Pixar computer scientists
created a program called Fizt,

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.810
which would automate the
movement of Sully's hairs

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.660
based on what's happening
in any given scene.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.650
Pixar would later use the same system

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.920
to simulate realistic
fur on animal characters,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.810
like Remy the rat and Dug the dog.

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.440
Every single Pixar movie brought

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.440
at least one groundbreaking innovation,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.910
each changing the future of animation

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.440
and moviemaking as a whole.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.320
Let's look at how every Pixar movie

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.903
brought animation into the future.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:43.350
Computer graphics were around
in the '80s in small doses,

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:46.220
but to make fully animated
movies like "Toy Story,"

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.830
the industry needed software

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.490
that could render complex animations.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.233
Rendering is when a computer takes

00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:53.908
all the info about an animation --

00:00:53.908 --> 00:00:56.441
the different algorithms
for color, motion,

00:00:56.441 --> 00:00:58.950
pattern, light, shadow, and effects --

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.000
and basically films the movie.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:02.833
In the late '80s,

00:01:02.833 --> 00:01:05.060
some of Pixar's top
researchers got together

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.510
to design RenderMan,

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.890
a program that combines all the 3D assets

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.470
created for each frame of a movie

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.278
and translates them into a film-quality,

00:01:13.278 --> 00:01:15.469
photorealistic final image.

00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:18.610
"Toy Story" wasn't the first
movie to use RenderMan,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:22.680
but it was the first-ever
3D-animated feature film.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:24.430
And the only way Pixar could render

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.430
the whole movie was with RenderMan,

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.590
which shows how this software
revolutionized animation.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.020
Since then, RenderMan
has been the final step

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.103
in the production pipeline

00:01:33.103 --> 00:01:35.544
behind every single Pixar film,

00:01:35.544 --> 00:01:38.130
and many non-Pixar films, too.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.210
In fact, it became the
industry standard for rendering

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.760
and the first software product
to be awarded an Oscar.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.370
In 1998, "A Bug's Life"
presented a different challenge:

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.480
creating characters with a smooth texture

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.000
and fluid, lifelike movements.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.410
For years, 3D models,

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.130
both in CG-animated
movies and video games,

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.270
had been mapped with polygons.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.640
That made some characters
come out looking patchy,

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.040
like Andy in "Toy Story,"

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.260
whose hand isn't smooth
like a human hand should be.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.430
So in "A Bug's Life,"

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.660
Pixar's artists applied
a modeling technique

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.860
they used on their Oscar-winning
short "Geri's Game."

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.070
This was called the subdivision technique,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.620
which one of Pixar's
founders helped invent.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.810
It's when a modeling artist
subdivides a hard-edged shape

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.317
enough times that it
appears as a smooth surface.

00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:28.320
"A Bug's Life" was the first feature film

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.180
to use subdivision, which
worked especially well

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.230
on hard-bodied insects like ants

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.471
to create glossy textures.

00:02:35.471 --> 00:02:38.700
And the team was able to
experiment with one soft creature,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.200
a Bavarian caterpillar named Heimlich.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.840
See all his smooth curves and rolls?

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.300
That's thanks to subdivision,
which made it possible

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.800
to create this type of plump, squishy bug.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.810
This technology would eventually be used

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.970
for almost every object in Pixar films,

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.167
and like RenderMan,

00:02:55.167 --> 00:02:58.240
subdivision became a standard
technique in the industry,

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.323
later helping to create CG characters

00:03:00.323 --> 00:03:01.870
in live-action movies,

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.778
like Gollum in the second and
third "Lord of the Rings."

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.790
If you look back at Andy in
the original "Toy Story,"

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.510
you'll notice his flesh
looks a little too similar

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.910
to the plastic surfaces on his toys.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.840
It lacks detail.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.500
For "Toy Story 2," Pixar
wanted to give Andy,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.683
his mom, and the new humans in the story

00:03:20.683 --> 00:03:22.291
more natural skin,

00:03:22.291 --> 00:03:25.450
so the studio created a
major addition to RenderMan:

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:26.450
a shader.

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:27.820
This added tool set helped

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.390
Pixar's shading artists
paint layers of detail

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.820
over the basic renderings of characters.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.460
Like real-life makeup artists,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.440
they could now give humans
unique skin characteristics

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.600
like pores, veins, sweat, redness,

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.290
whiteheads, moles, whiskers,
or a 5 o'clock shadow,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:50.303
making them look less
plastic and more alive.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.650
Pixar had to create
some very furry monsters

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:55.700
for "Monsters, Inc.,"

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.170
including the 8-foot-tall Sullivan,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:01.000
who had nearly 3 million
hairs on his body.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.440
Animating every strand of hair one by one

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.230
would've been impossible,

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:08.720
especially since Sully
appears in over 600 shots.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.240
So Pixar set up a department
dedicated to simulation,

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:13.500
a way to automate the movement

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.220
of elements like hair, fur, and clothing.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:19.350
The team also built a
simulator engine called Fizt,

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.910
which calculates, for example,

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:23.340
how hair moves with a
character's movements

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.240
or how hairs collectively respond

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.400
to forces like gravity, wind, or snow.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:29.775
Pixar has used Fizt

00:04:29.775 --> 00:04:31.540
on every one of its movies since then

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:32.848
to give realistic movement

00:04:32.848 --> 00:04:34.860
to everything from human flesh

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.277
to the suckers on an octopus.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.890
Pixar moved underwater for "Finding Nemo,"

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:44.130
so the studio had to nail the appearance

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.080
of light passing through
objects in the ocean,

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:50.500
like the several thousand
jellyfish in this sequence.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.440
These jellies called for different shading

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.150
than any other creature
Pixar had worked on.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:57.930
As CG supervisor Lisa Forssell said,

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.380
the team didn't want the jellyfish

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.290
to look transparent, like a window,

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.890
or translucent, like a shower curtain.

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:06.630
Their membrane needed to
resemble bathroom glass,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.020
where "you can see through it,

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.370
but it's all distorted and blurry."

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.870
So Pixar wrote a whole new shading system

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.590
called Transblurrency,

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:16.580
which blurred the background of objects

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.190
based on their depth and
distance from the viewer,

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.860
calculating camera position
and optical light paths

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:23.240
into the equation.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.290
You can see how the
background is most blurred

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.540
in the middle of the jellyfish

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.270
and least blurred on
the edges of its bell.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:30.870
That's true to the way light refracts

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.480
through a jellyfish's membrane,

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:34.750
and it captures that
bathroom-window effect

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:36.417
they were going for.

00:05:37.977 --> 00:05:40.380
"The Incredibles" was Pixar's first movie

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.380
with an entirely human cast of characters,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.590
and when it came to
building those characters,

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:49.690
none of them were harder,
literally, than this one.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.530
Bob, better known as Mr. Incredible,

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:57.330
would be the most muscular
character Pixar had ever created.

00:05:57.330 --> 00:06:00.210
So the studio came up with
a completely new approach

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.130
to his layers of
skeleton, muscle, and fat.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.330
The team designed a type
of muscle rig called Goo

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:07.638
that let a character's skin

00:06:07.638 --> 00:06:09.990
respond to their moving muscles.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.380
Better yet, animators could
see this response in real time

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:15.720
as they moved their character around.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.500
This led to breakthroughs in body parts

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.030
that had always been
problematic in animation,

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:21.430
like the shoulders.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:22.310
Until this point,

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:23.840
Pixar had stuck with characters

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.900
that have more primitive shoulders.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:29.010
Look at Buzz, whose shoulder
consists of a ball and socket,

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.840
or Woody, who just has a stitch

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.800
where his arm hinges off his body.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.880
Using Goo, animators could
master the tiny details

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:39.260
of Mr. Incredible's much more
complex shoulder movement,

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:43.080
like how his trapezius muscle
affects his pectoral muscle.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.590
Once the rigging and
modeling team perfected Bob,

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:47.480
they could use his skeleton as a template,

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.860
reshaping the muscle rig
to fit other characters

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.110
and giving them equally complex motion.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:56.450
In real life, a car would look weird

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.010
if it didn't have any
reflections on its surface.

00:06:59.010 --> 00:07:02.220
But translating this on
screen can get complicated,

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.630
especially for a movie like "Cars."

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:07.570
See, shiny cars reflect other shiny cars,

00:07:07.570 --> 00:07:10.010
which makes for a lot
of reflections at play

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:11.850
in scenes like this one.

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:13.460
The only way Pixar could master

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.335
all these reflective metal surfaces

00:07:15.335 --> 00:07:16.860
was with ray tracing,

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:18.235
a technique of mapping out

00:07:18.235 --> 00:07:19.743
all the rays of light in a scene

00:07:19.743 --> 00:07:23.000
as they bounce off walls,
objects, and characters.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.516
Pixar first used ray tracing

00:07:24.516 --> 00:07:26.049
in two shots of "A Bug's Life"

00:07:26.049 --> 00:07:28.560
to create the reflections
on a glass bottle,

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.350
but it only became a main
part of the rendering process

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:34.240
with "Cars," where it was used
to create the sharp shadows

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:37.210
and detailed reflections
the filmmakers needed.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.970
Ray tracing transformed animation.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:43.410
It even ended up on CGI
elements in live-action movies,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:46.730
like this Transformer or Iron Man's suit.

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:49.080
And it set the stage for
even more complex lighting

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:52.405
in future Pixar movies,
like "Monsters University."

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:55.070
In "Ratatouille,"

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:59.180
all the dishes had to
look absolutely delicious.

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.250
So the shading, art, and lighting teams

00:08:01.250 --> 00:08:03.340
studied food photography to figure out

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:06.740
what exactly makes food
look good on camera.

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.210
They determined that a lot
of a dish's visual appeal

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:11.930
boils down to the appearance of softness,

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.650
which comes from light
passing through the food.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.690
See, a lot of foods are
actually kind of translucent,

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:19.110
which means some light will go

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:20.420
through the surface of the material,

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.000
scatter around, and then reflect back.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.540
Pixar already had a way of
replicating this effect,

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:27.760
a technique for rendering
translucent materials

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.780
called subsurface scattering.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.070
This tool modeled light
scattering outwards,

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.060
through the surface of the food.

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:35.810
But the filmmakers were still going

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:37.650
for a more translucent look

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:38.752
and wanted to show light

00:08:38.752 --> 00:08:41.620
going straight through the food as well.

00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:44.490
So they developed a new
type of light called Gummi,

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.850
based partly on the
gummy-bear-like quality

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:49.400
of Marlin and Nemo in "Finding Nemo."

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:51.880
Take this shot of cheese,
grapes, and bread.

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.500
The cheese in this first
shot has no lighting.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.302
With the subsurface scattering,

00:08:56.302 --> 00:08:59.460
you can see the outer edges
and top layers illuminated.

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:00.890
And finally, with Gummi,

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.190
the light permeates
every layer of the cheese

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:05.910
to give it a soft, diffused glow.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.400
They used this approach on entire shelves

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:09.970
of cheese and vegetables,

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:12.330
as well as on liquids like wine.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.600
Together, subsurface scattering and Gummi

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.916
created a two-part translucency effect

00:09:16.916 --> 00:09:18.858
that made sure no food item would look

00:09:18.858 --> 00:09:22.009
waxy, hard, or plastic on screen.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:25.164
On the old Pixar camera,

00:09:25.164 --> 00:09:28.161
any tilt or pan was a two-dimensional move

00:09:28.161 --> 00:09:30.440
without any change in perspective.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:32.173
But "WALL-E"'s filmmakers wanted

00:09:32.173 --> 00:09:33.856
the viewer's perspective to shift

00:09:33.856 --> 00:09:35.320
when the virtual camera did,

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:38.800
so it'd feel like the camera
was actually moving in space.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:40.910
So they rebuilt their camera system,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.460
modeling it on the way an
anamorphic camera would move

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.670
and consulting with a
live-action cinematographer,

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:49.240
a first for any Pixar film.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.570
The first act of the movie went
for a loose, handheld feel,

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:53.880
designed to look like it was shot

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.060
from a camera operator's shoulder.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.110
The second and third
acts are supposed to look

00:09:58.110 --> 00:09:59.940
like they're shot with a Steadicam.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:02.840
And since a camera never
moves perfectly in real life,

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:04.810
director Andrew Stanton said his team

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:07.440
spent "probably 90%" of their time

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.640
putting little imperfections
into the virtual camera.

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:12.710
There are shots where the focus lags,

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:15.790
tracking shots with a little
bump in the camera movement,

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:17.100
and scenes where it feels

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.050
like the cameraperson sees something

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.170
and catches it a few frames later.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:24.050
The result is perfectly imperfect.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:28.230
In "Up," Carl's canopy of balloons

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:30.130
is central to the storyline.

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.730
It involved thousands
of colorful balloons,

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.800
each attached to the chimney
of Carl's house by a string,

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:39.060
with thousands of collisions
going on at any given moment.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.150
And when one balloon bumped into another,

00:10:41.150 --> 00:10:44.570
the second one would bump into
a third balloon, and so on.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.620
At the time, Pixar's physical simulator

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:49.300
could only handle about 500 balloons,

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:51.360
so the effects department
wrote a new program

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:53.240
to run under the simulator.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:54.940
Using Newton's laws of motion,

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:56.580
the computer accounted for forces

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.740
acting on the balloons in every shot

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.560
and then decided how
each balloon should move.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.050
It also made sure that all
the balloons were buoyant,

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:05.630
that their strings were attached,

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:07.350
and that the wind was blowing through them

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:11.880
in a realistic way for every
single frame of animation.

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.650
Ultimately, Pixar's
simulator was powerful enough

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:19.310
to handle 10,297 balloons.

00:11:20.997 --> 00:11:22.522
"Toy Story 3" brought together

00:11:22.522 --> 00:11:24.405
a bigger cast of characters --

00:11:24.405 --> 00:11:27.610
three times as many as
in the original movie.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.850
Costuming all of them wouldn't be easy,

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.310
especially when introducing
characters like Bonnie,

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.460
who wears three different
outfits in the movie,

00:11:34.460 --> 00:11:38.170
or Ken, who changes
costumes in every scene.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:39.003
To dress them all,

00:11:39.003 --> 00:11:41.940
Pixar needed a faster
way of making clothes.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:43.390
Traditionally, the studio used

00:11:43.390 --> 00:11:45.415
a 2D garment-tailoring system

00:11:45.415 --> 00:11:48.370
that required artists with
special tailoring skills

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:49.810
to cut out pieces of cloth

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:52.147
and stitch them together in the computer.

00:11:52.147 --> 00:11:55.300
"Toy Story 3" used a new 3D cloth system

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.080
that let any modeling artists,

00:11:57.080 --> 00:11:59.160
not just expert digital tailors,

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:02.030
sculpt garments like a tailor
would in the real world

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.610
and then turn those garments
into simulatable clothes.

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:07.470
Since more modelers could build garments,

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:08.962
Pixar's artists could dress

00:12:08.962 --> 00:12:12.787
over 300 animated characters
for "Toy Story 3,"

00:12:12.787 --> 00:12:15.773
compared to the 76 in the first movie.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:20.820
The filmmakers behind
"Cars 2" envisioned it

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:24.010
as a "Jason Bourne"-style spy movie

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:25.960
where the cars would race around cities

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.140
from London to Tokyo.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.870
They wanted to reproduce those settings

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:31.040
with a level of detail

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:35.290
that was 10 times what Pixar
had put into any other film.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.580
For one of the movie's biggest sequences,

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:39.740
that meant making specific improvements

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:42.550
to the way Pixar depicted waves.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:44.110
Technology from "Finding Nemo"

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:46.600
was best suited for calmer waters,

00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:49.150
but in "Cars 2," the
filmmakers wanted to show

00:12:49.150 --> 00:12:53.180
rough, high waves slamming
into the sides of the boat.

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:56.600
They came up with an ocean
generator called Tessendorf,

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:00.240
which used algorithms to
simulate sharper, "cuspier" waves

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:03.970
that captured the danger of
an ocean's choppy surface.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:05.910
With this, Pixar could better convey

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:07.920
the physics of the natural world

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:11.350
and pushed its water
simulation to the next level.

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.210
Detailed, realistic simulation

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.160
became the company's calling card,

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:18.150
continuing with the curly hair in "Brave."

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.700
We'll get to that in part two.

